# Patch 0086 — Tab history TREE + the saved-session manager (ROADMAP Phase 7;
# FEATURES 7.1 and 7.2).
#
# THE PROBLEM IS ONE SENTENCE. Every browser's session history is a list with
# a cursor: go back three pages, follow a different link, and the three pages
# you came from are GONE — silently truncated, with no gesture anywhere that
# brings them back. That is the most common way people lose their place while
# researching, and it is a data-model choice, not a law of browsers.
#
# THIS DOES NOT REWIRE BACK AND FORWARD. Gecko's session history keeps its
# exact meaning, because changing what Back does would break muscle memory
# every user already has. The tree is recorded ALONGSIDE it and offers the
# abandoned branches back. Jumping to a node is an ORDINARY navigation, which
# is what keeps the model honest: the next location change is classified by
# the same rules as any other, so the cursor visibly lands where it should.
#
# CLASSIFICATION, in order, against the node the tab currently sits on: same
# URL is a reload (refresh the title, move nothing); ANY ANCESTOR's URL is a
# BACK; a child's URL is a FORWARD; anything else is a new node parented at
# the cursor. Back walks the whole ancestor chain rather than checking only
# the parent, because jumping several entries at once is a real gesture (the
# Back button's dropdown, a long press) and checking one level would classify
# it as a brand new page and duplicate a node the tree already holds. Forward
# stays one level: the ancestor chain is bounded, descendants are not. That last case is the entire feature — if the cursor had moved
# up first, the new node is a SECOND CHILD, and the branch that Gecko would
# have truncated is now simply a sibling.
#
# TRIMMING ONLY EVER DROPS LEAVES, oldest first, and never the cursor or
# anything on its path to the root. Dropping an interior node would orphan a
# whole branch, which is the one thing this module exists to prevent.
#
# PERSISTENCE RIDES SESSIONSTORE (setCustomTabValue), deliberately: per tab,
# survives a restart, dies when the tab is closed for good. A tree that
# outlived its tab would be a second and worse history store; one that did not
# survive a restart would lose the branch exactly when it is most wanted.
#
# THE PANEL IS PER-TAB, WHICH IS WHY IT IS A PANEL. An about: page would have
# to be opened in some other tab and then reach back for the one the user
# actually meant.
#
# SAVED SESSIONS (7.2) close what patch 0081 opened. That patch shipped "Save
# This Research Session" and stopped: a saved session could only be found
# through the timeline of the ONE Space it belonged to, which is exactly what
# a person forgets three weeks later. This lists every saved session across
# every Space and adds rename and delete. DELETE MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS:
# retention deliberately exempts labelled snapshots (0081, correctly — silent
# expiry of something the user named is data loss), so without this a saved
# session was permanent. It confirms first, for the same reason.
#
# Restore still goes through kavachaBranchSpace, so it stays NON-DESTRUCTIVE
# (ADR 0006): restoring a session forks a branch and never overwrites the
# present. One mechanism, not two.
#
# The two panel titles are ATTRIBUTE-form Fluent messages, because a
# value-only message on a XUL label renders blank — patch 0067 fixed thirteen
# of those, every one of which a geometry probe had called present.
#
